Q: How do I make it so that the script responds differently for each element? A: <p>The easiest way to do this is given your specific code to traverse the document object model to the next sibling from the button that was clicked when opening the modal. Upon closing, traverse the DOM upwards to find the <code>&lt;div class=&quot;modal&quot;&gt;</code> element.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;html&gt;
    &lt;style&gt;
        body{
            background-color: #7777ff;
        }

        .modal{
            display: none;
            position:fixed;
            top:0;
            bottom:0;
            left:0;
            right:0;
            background-color: #ffff77;
        }
        .window{
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            margin:auto;
            height: 33.3%;
            width: 20%;
            background-color: aqua;
            align-items: center;
        }
        .header{
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: row;
            width:100%;
            button{
                padding:0px;
            };
        }
    &lt;/style&gt;
    &lt;body&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            &lt;button onclick="openM(this)"&gt;&lt;br&gt;a&lt;/button&gt;
            &lt;div class="modal"&gt;
                &lt;div class="window"&gt;
                    &lt;div class="header"&gt;
                        &lt;div style="flex:1;"&gt;titel a&lt;/div&gt;
                        &lt;button onclick="closeM(this)"&gt;x&lt;/button&gt;
                    &lt;/div&gt;
                    &lt;p&gt;placeholder a&lt;/p&gt;
                &lt;/div&gt;
             &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;div&gt;
            &lt;button onclick="openM(this)"&gt;&lt;br&gt;b&lt;/button&gt;
            &lt;div class="modal"&gt;
                &lt;div class="window"&gt;
                    &lt;div class="header"&gt;
                        &lt;div style="flex:1;"&gt;titel b&lt;/div&gt;
                        &lt;button onclick="closeM(this)"&gt;x&lt;/button&gt;
                    &lt;/div&gt;
                    &lt;p&gt;placeholder b&lt;/p&gt;
                &lt;/div&gt;
             &lt;/div&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/body&gt;
    &lt;script&gt;
        function openM(button){
            const modal = button.nextElementSibling;

            modal.style.display = "flex";
        }
        function closeM(button){
            const modal = button.closest('div.modal');

            modal.style.display = "none";
        }
    &lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Implementation notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Remove the redundant <code>id</code> attribute. The <code>id</code> attribute must be unique within the document, and serves no useful purpose.</li>
<li>Use the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/nextElementSibling" rel="nofollow noreferrer">nextElementSibling</a> property to get the <code>&lt;div class=&quot;model&quot;&gt;</code> element that follows the button in the source code.</li>
<li>The <code>this</code> variable inside inline event handlers is a pointer to the element being clicked. This gives you a reference point within the DOM to begin traversal (see <code>openM(this)</code> and <code>closeM(this)</code> in the HTML).</li>
<li>Use the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/closest" rel="nofollow noreferrer">closest()</a> method on an element to pass a CSS selector that grabs the nearest ancestor. This is akin to the jQuery.closest() method.</li>
</ul>

Q: Budget app problems A: <p>The blank screen is most likely unrelated to the balance calculation itself. Make sure each Activity loads its layout inside <code>onCreate()</code>:</p>
<pre><code>@Override
protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
    setContentView(R.layout.your_layout);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Also check that every Activity is declared in your <code>AndroidManifest.xml</code>.</p>
<p>For the balance, I wouldn't pass the current balance back and forth between the deposit and withdrawal Activities. Keep a single source of truth for the transactions/balance (database, repository, etc.), update it when a deposit or withdrawal is made, then read the updated balance when the main Activity resumes.</p>
<p>For a simple prototype, you could pass values with Intent extras, but once financial data needs to persist across sessions, storing the transactions and deriving the balance from them is a much cleaner approach.</p>

Q: Return on result set from many tables using a cursor A: <p>You don't need a cursor here. Don't practice with it, it's a waste of time. They are slow, inefficient, and nearly always unnecessary.</p>
<p>Instead use set-based logic, by placing your conditions in a table variable or Table Valued Parameter</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>DECLARE @deps TABLE (dep nvarchar(100) PRIMARY KEY);
INSERT @deps (dep)
VALUES ('Computer Studies'), ('Civil Engineering');

SELECT *
FROM dbo.DEPARTMENTS d
WHERE d.DepartmentName IN (
    SELECT t.dep
    FROM @deps t
);
</code></pre>

Q: Running a Class.method() when that module is imported A: <p>The class has several ways of running methods when a class is defined - which is usually when the module it is defined in is imported (but also, if it is declared inside a function, for example, when the function is called).</p>
<p>Hstorically, one would have to write a custom metaclass for that, and them have code in this metaclass <code>__init__</code> or <code>__new__</code> methods to call your &quot;bootstrap method&quot; (or just do the bootstrapping themselves). Since Python 3.6, howeverm the <code>__init_subclass__</code> special method has been defined to do exactly that, without needing a custom metaclass.</p>
<p>You have to define it in a regular superclass, and have your class that wants code to be run when it is defined, to inherit from that class. It is a classmethod, called with <code>cls</code> as the first parameter, and whatever keyword arguments you put when defining the class, on the <code>class</code> statement itself, and should, ordinarilly, call the superclass' <code>__init_subclass__</code> to ensure proper class composition while keeping this feature working for all your classes.</p>
<pre><code>class Base:
    def __init_subclass__(cls):
        cls.onLoad()

class Class1(Base):
    var1 = 0

    @classmethod
    def onLoad(cls):
        cls.var1 = 1
</code></pre>
<p>Other than that, there is nothing wrong with your approach of just calling a classmethod at the top level of the module, just after the class definition, as you did in the snippet in the question. It would be nice to have a comment next to it explaining why it is needed, but that is all.</p>
